  70:     `bus.on('search', (values) => â€¦)` after Search; `bus.on('reset', () => â€¦)` after Reset. `bus.emit(type, detail)` sends one.
  71:     Plain inputs (text boxes, radios, checkboxes) are not on the bus: listen to their DOM events.
  72:   - `openDialog('d2')`: open a captured dialog from code (e.g. a radio that opens one).
  73:   - `data`: the kit.json (`widgets`, `dialogs`, `grids`, `search`, `row`, `plan`, `missing`).
  74: - Services (`src/services/<screen>.ts`): `search(values)` returns `{ total, rows }` of synthetic rows; `save(dialog, values)`.
  75:   `endpoints` names the live endpoints. Edit freely: it is generated once.
  76: - Attributes the kit acts on (keep them): `data-kit-action="search|reset|cancel|create|save|close|collapse|menu|menu-close"`,
  77:   `data-kit-tip="text"` (a tooltip), `data-kit-h` (hidden at rest; Search reveals the ones in `search.reveals`),
  78:   `data-kit-collapse` (a panel body), `data-kit-menu*` (the side menu: never touch it from a screen).
  79: 
  80: Worked example: the FTP Server Credentials screen needs no code of its own beyond the port. Its Create New opens dialog `d1`
  81: ("Add FTP Server Credentials", ported to `dialogs/DialogD1.tsx`), its tooltips and validation come from the kit, and its grid
  82: fills on Search. A screen with a conditional field adds one small component next to the markup, for example:
  83: 
  84: ```tsx
  85: // shows the part hidden at rest (data-kit-h="h4") only for "Run Monthly", as the screen's script does
  86: function RunSchedule() {
  87:   useEffect(() => {
  88:     const on = (e: Event) => {
  89:       const r = e.target as HTMLInputElement;
  90:       if (r.name === 'RunSchedule') document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-kit-h="h4"]')!.style.display = r.value === 'M' ? '' : 'none';
  91:     };
  92:     document.addEventListener('change', on);
  93:     return () => document.removeEventListener('change', on);
  94:   }, []);
  95:   return null;
  96: }
  97: ```
  98: 
  99: ## The side menu (the shell, built by the kit)
 100: 
 101: Measured on the live portal (docs/provider-probe, probe 2): the hamburger swaps the menu root's classes (`display-none` â†’
 102: `display-flex`, which are `!important`) and appends `<div class="disabled-backdrop">` to `<body>`; there is no fade. A module
 103: flies out to the left after Kendo's hover delay (100 ms) with its menu animation (open 200 ms, close 100 ms). Only the X and a
 104: menu link close it: Escape and a click on the backdrop do not. The capture records all of this on the menu root
 105: (`data-kit-menu-add/-remove/-backdrop/-hover/-open-ms/-close-ms`) and the kit replays it; a link to a rebuilt screen routes there.
 106: 
 107: A module's submenu is a box **beside** the module list, not inside it: Kendo lifts the popup out of the `<ul>` (which clips what
 108: is inside) into a `.k-animation-container` on the menu's scroll wrapper, `left: -width; top: <the item's top>` for the vertical
 109: side menu, under the item for the horizontal **module tab** (`#activeModuleMenu`, "CLAIMS MANAGEMENT âˆ¨" under the header). The
 110: kit does the same for both (`[data-kit-flyout]`), with each menu's own timings (stamped on the `<ul>`). Only a link to a screen of
 111: this app navigates (and closes the menu); every other item is shown and leads nowhere, so the whole menu can be browsed. **Quick
 112: Links** (`#mobileMenuIcon`) is a collapsible shell panel: the capture stamps it (`collapse`) and the kit toggles its angle icon,
 113: `subpanel-collapsed`, title and body like the portal's `expandPanel`. The **page help book** (`.help-icon-container`) belongs to
 114: the page, not the shell: each screen's `screen.json` `help` is its markup or null, and the screen shows or hides it.
 115: `kit/states` check all of it on the rebuild (`menu-open`, `menu-flyout`, `tab-flyout`, `menu-close`): the fly-out must be on top and
 116: outside the list, and look like the live one. Captures from before this have no `menu-flyout`/`tab-flyout`/`help`: capture again.
 117: 
 118: ## Dialogs and grid rows
 119: 
 120: The capture reads the screen's own script for `openModalOverlay("<panel>", â€¦)` (each dialog, and the button that opens it) and
 121: `setKendoGridDataBoundEvents(e, msg, onClick, onDblClick)` (62 of 89 menu pages open a row on double-click). It clicks each
 122: opener with nothing on top of it, waits for the window while the page loads (up to 20 s), and double-clicks the first row when
 123: the script says so. In the rebuild a click selects the row and a double-click opens its dialog. A dialog the script opens but
 124: the capture could not see is listed in `screen.json` `missing` and in facts.md as `[UNKNOWN]`: never invent it.
 125: 
